 "descriptionModule": {
  "briefSummary": "This study tests whether a single dose of nicotine gum changes brain responses to sounds and the ability to detect target sounds in non-smoking adults with chronic tinnitus. In a double-blind, placebo-controlled, within-subject crossover design, each participant attends two sessions and receives a single 4 mg dose of nicotine polacrilex gum at one session and a flavor-matched placebo gum at the other, in random order. After gum administration, EEG is recorded at rest and during an active auditory oddball task. The primary measures are the P3 brain response (amplitude and latency) and oddball task accuracy and reaction time.",
  "detailedDescription": "Relationship to prior registration: This trial is a separate, newly enrolled study conducted under the same IRB-approved protocol (San Diego State University IRB-25-0430) as the completed trial NCT07772089, which evaluated acute nicotine gum effects on tinnitus loudness and selective attention. Participants and data in this trial are collected independently of NCT07772089.\n\nPopulation: Non-smoking adults aged 18-80 years with chronic subjective tinnitus, using the same eligibility criteria as NCT07772089.\n\nIntervention and administration: A single 4 mg dose of nicotine polacrilex gum or flavor-matched placebo gum, chewed continuously for 20 minutes at one chew per second (paced by a 1 Hz auditory cue) with saliva retained, following the administration protocol established in the investigators' gum optimization study.\n\nDesign: Randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, within-subject crossover with two sessions on separate days separated by a washout of at least 72 hours. Session order is randomized 1:1 using a computer-generated sequence; allocation is concealed with sequentially numbered, opaque, sealed envelopes; gum is prepared by an independent examiner and participants are blindfolded during administration.\n\nProcedures: At the start of the first session, pure-tone audiometry, the Tinnitus Handicap Inventory, the Tinnitus Functional Index, and a pre-administration tinnitus loudness match are completed. In each session, tinnitus loudness is matched before and after gum administration. All EEG and oddball measures are obtained after gum administration only. EEG is analyzed at electrodes Fz, Cz, Pz, and Oz: resting-state spectral power (delta, theta, alpha, beta, gamma) and aperiodic (1/f) slope, and P3 amplitude and latency to target tones in an active auditory oddball task, with concurrent behavioral accuracy, reaction time, and d'."
 },
